          "heading": "Theoretical Overview: Signals, Spectral Analysis, and Baseband Transmission Channels",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Communication system architecture and link budgets\n\n**Learning objective:** Translate an end-to-end information requirement into signal, channel, synchronization and receiver constraints.\n\n## Physical perspective\n\nA communication design has several boundaries: a message source, an encoder, a waveform mapper, a physical channel, and a receiver making decisions. A symbol error is not identical to a bit error, and neither is a packet outage. Each result must be evaluated at its own boundary. The unit develops power budgets before treating a signal as a clean mathematical curve; a beautiful demodulated waveform is not meaningful without a noise, bandwidth and calibration budget.\n\n## Extended engineering case: how to reason from requirements\n\nA rural environmental telemetry system demonstrates why requirements come before waveforms. A sensor generates a small packet once each minute. Its payload rate averaged over an hour is tiny, yet a long preamble and the receiver's wake-up schedule can dominate battery energy. A high raw bit rate may reduce on-air time, but it can demand more instantaneous power, better signal-to-noise ratio and a more accurate oscillator. To size the supply, integrate electrical current over each state: acquisition, processing, transmit, listen and sleep. To size the communication channel, calculate packet error probability under the worst link margin, not merely at the average received level. Two independent retries do not guarantee success when outages are correlated by the same deep fade. A useful design table records payload length, header length, channel code, symbol rate, transmitter efficiency, antenna height, range and receiver noise figure. The first budget uses free-space conditions; a second budget adds placement and fade uncertainty. The engineer should label these as assumptions rather than observations. Finally, decide whether the application needs acknowledgements or can tolerate an occasional missing environmental reading. The answer affects the radio architecture as much as the modulation scheme.\n\nA contrasting indoor video link has a very different bottleneck. Large bursts require sustained throughput, low delay and a channel that changes with moving bodies and walls. A single headline 'range' cannot compare the two products. Indoor reflections create frequency-selective behavior, while high peak-to-average traffic may stress buffers and burst scheduling. Measure receiver sensitivity at a declared packet-error criterion and payload throughput at a declared layer of the protocol stack. When asked whether an extra 3 dB of transmitter power doubles range, explain that the result depends on propagation exponent, interference, legal power limits, antenna orientation and whether coverage is interference- or noise-limited. In an ideal free-space budget a 3 dB increase multiplies range by about the square root of two, not by two.\n\n## Connections and study order\n\nThe next four lessons move from the simplest tractable model to a broader engineering treatment of this unit. Solve their worked cases separately first, then assemble the assumptions in a single coherent link design. For every step, distinguish data representing the actual message from a signal generated by the transceiver and from noise introduced by the environment or receiver. Use the model's limiting cases to decide whether parameters can be safely neglected. Finally, specify how any simulation could be validated through an independent analytic calculation and, if qualified laboratory access exists, through a supervised physical measurement.\n\n## Unit lessons\n\n- [1.1 The end-to-end communication chain](../lessons/U01-L01-the-end-to-end-communication-chain.html) \u2014 model `information rate Rb, symbol rate Rs=Rb/log2(M) for uncoded fixed-length M-ary mapping`\n- [1.2 Power, gains and decibels](../lessons/U01-L02-power-gains-and-decibels.html) \u2014 model `G_dB=10 log10(Pout/Pin); G_dB=20 log10(Vout/Vin) only for equal impedances`\n- [1.3 Free-space propagation and received power](../lessons/U01-L03-free-space-propagation-and-received-power.html) \u2014 model `Pr=Pt Gt Gr (lambda/(4 pi R))^2 under matched free-space far-field conditions`\n- [1.4 Noise temperature, SNR and implementation margin](../lessons/U01-L04-noise-temperature-snr-and-implementation-margin.html) \u2014 model `N=kTB; N(dBm)\u2248-174 dBm/Hz+10 log10(B_Hz)+NF_dB near 290 K`\n\n## Unit assessment and project\n\n- [Self-assessment](../assessments/unit-01.html)\n- [Integrated project](../projects/unit-01.html)\n- [Interactive model](../simulations/model-01.html)\n"
