          "heading": "Lesson 1.2 Power, gains and decibels",
          "content": "# Lesson 1.2 Power, gains and decibels\n\n**Unit:** 1 \u2014 Communication system architecture and link budgets  \n**Core model:** `G_dB=10 log10(Pout/Pin); G_dB=20 log10(Vout/Vin) only for equal impedances`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nA decibel is a logarithmic ratio and not an absolute power. Express absolute levels as dBm relative to 1 mW or dBW relative to 1 W. Add losses and gains only after establishing compatible reference planes. Cable loss, filter insertion loss, amplifier noise figure, mismatch and antenna gain affect different aspects of an end-to-end budget. Antenna gain concentrates radiation rather than generating electrical power. At every reference point indicate whether the power is available, incident, accepted or delivered.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **power, gains and decibels**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `G_dB=10 log10(Pout/Pin); G_dB=20 log10(Vout/Vin) only for equal impedances`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nA source of 0 dBm passes a 3 dB insertion loss and a 15 dB amplifier: output is nominally +12 dBm (about 15.8 mW). If the load is mismatched, delivered power will be lower than this simple matched-chain estimate.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nUsing 20 log for a power ratio doubles the answer incorrectly. Using 10 log for a voltage ratio without specifying the impedance is also incomplete.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nTrace a budget across transmitter, feed line, two antennas, path attenuation, receiver filter and detector. Include a column naming each physical reference plane.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** Using 20 log for a power ratio doubles the answer incorrectly. Using 10 log for a voltage ratio without specifying the impedance is also incomplete. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Trace a budget across transmitter, feed line, two antennas, path attenuation, receiver filter and detector. Include a column naming each physical reference plane. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **power, gains and decibels** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
