          "heading": "Lesson 1.3 Free-space propagation and received power",
          "content": "# Lesson 1.3 Free-space propagation and received power\n\n**Unit:** 1 \u2014 Communication system architecture and link budgets  \n**Core model:** `Pr=Pt Gt Gr (lambda/(4 pi R))^2 under matched free-space far-field conditions`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nThe Friis relationship combines geometric spreading with transmit and receive antenna gains. It assumes line-of-sight free space, polarization alignment, the antenna far field and matched reference conditions. Propagation loss is frequency dependent when comparing fixed-gain antennas, but frequency scaling requires care when holding physical antenna aperture fixed. The popular free-space path loss expression is a ratio of transmitted and received powers at defined antenna terminals, not a universal promise of coverage.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **free-space propagation and received power**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `Pr=Pt Gt Gr (lambda/(4 pi R))^2 under matched free-space far-field conditions`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nAt 2.4 GHz and 100 m, wavelength is about 0.125 m. Free-space path loss is 20 log10(4 pi times 100/0.125), approximately 80.05 dB. With +20 dBm transmit, 2 dBi gains at both ends, and 2 dB feeder loss total, received power is approximately -58.05 dBm.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nUrban multipath, absorption, shadowing and antenna misalignment make an unqualified free-space result optimistic. Keep fading margin separate from a deterministic Friis calculation.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nCompare 100 m and 200 m distances at the same carrier and geometry. Explain why doubling distance adds about 6.02 dB of free-space path loss.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** Urban multipath, absorption, shadowing and antenna misalignment make an unqualified free-space result optimistic. Keep fading margin separate from a deterministic Friis calculation. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Compare 100 m and 200 m distances at the same carrier and geometry. Explain why doubling distance adds about 6.02 dB of free-space path loss. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **free-space propagation and received power** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
