          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Signals, Spectral Analysis, and Baseband Transmission Channels",
          "content": "# Unit 1 project \u2014 Communication system architecture and link budgets: an auditable synthetic design study\n\n**Problem statement.** Translate an end-to-end information requirement into signal, channel, synchronization and receiver constraints. The deliverable is a self-contained engineering analysis, not a claim that a device was measured or certified.\n\n## Scenario\n\nA rural environmental telemetry system demonstrates why requirements come before waveforms. A sensor generates a small packet once each minute. Its payload rate averaged over an hour is tiny, yet a long preamble and the receiver's wake-up schedule can dominate battery energy. A high raw bit rate may reduce on-air time, but it can demand more instantaneous power, better signal-to-noise ratio and a more accurate oscillator. To size the supply, integrate electrical current over each state: acquisition, processing, transmit, listen and sleep. To size the communication channel, calculate packet error probability under the worst link margin, not merely at the average received level. Two independent retries do not guarantee success when outages are correlated by the same deep fade. A useful design table records payload length, header length, channel code, symbol rate, transmitter efficiency, antenna height, range and receiver noise figure. The first budget uses free-space conditions; a second budget adds placement and fade uncertainty. The engineer should label these as assumptions rather than observations. Finally, decide whether the application needs acknowledgements or can tolerate an occasional missing environmental reading. The answer affects the radio architecture as much as the modulation scheme.\n\n## Required work products\n\n1. **Requirements document.** State the message type, sample or symbol rate, occupied-bandwidth convention, reference impedances or powers and a numerical reliability criterion. Differentiate functional requirements (what users need) from implementation choices (how blocks operate).\n2. **Equation notebook.** For each of the unit's four governing relationships, derive the formula assumptions, provide an annotated worked calculation and evaluate a limiting case. Keep all intermediate calculations in linear units until a logarithmic representation is actually necessary.\n3. **Simulation experiment.** Open the associated synthetic model. Take screenshots or download CSV for three input settings: nominal, stressed, and nearly ideal. Record why each setting was chosen. Compare at least two numerical points with a direct formula calculation independent of the browser code.\n4. **Failure analysis.** Write one paragraph each on systematic bias, random variation and model mismatch. State the measurement instrument or independent experiment needed to distinguish the alternatives in a supervised hardware implementation.\n5. **Decision memo.** Recommend a configuration for the stated application. Include a tradeoff table of bandwidth, power, complexity, delay, expected errors and uncertainty. Explicitly identify the two most consequential unverified assumptions.\n\n## Suggested numerical investigation\n\n- **Experiment 1, The end-to-end communication chain:** Specify a voice, sensor and file-transfer link separately. For each, identify acceptable delay, residual error, data rate, operating range and which block enforces the requirement. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 2, Power, gains and decibels:** Trace a budget across transmitter, feed line, two antennas, path attenuation, receiver filter and detector. Include a column naming each physical reference plane. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 3, Free-space propagation and received power:** Compare 100 m and 200 m distances at the same carrier and geometry. Explain why doubling distance adds about 6.02 dB of free-space path loss. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 4, Noise temperature, SNR and implementation margin:** Recompute noise floor when noise bandwidth is quadrupled. Explain the six-decibel effect and why narrowing a filter too far distorts the wanted waveform. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n\n## Evaluation rubric (100 points)\n\n- Model derivations and units: 25 points. A formula with unexplained symbols does not earn full marks.\n- Independent arithmetic and numerical consistency: 20 points. Reproduce at least one graph point without reusing the graph's implementation.\n- Simulation experiment and transparent provenance: 20 points. Identify every result as synthetic.\n- Failure analysis and sensitivity: 20 points. Include meaningful nonideal tests, not only cosmetic parameter changes.\n- Clear engineering recommendation: 15 points. Tie the recommendation to the defined objective and state where further evidence is required.\n\n**Safety:** do not build or operate radio transmitters, powered antennas or high-voltage test apparatus as part of this offline project.\n"
