          "heading": "Theoretical Overview: Amplitude Modulation: DSB-FC, DSB-SC, SSB, and VSB Schemes",
          "content": "# Unit 2: Signals, spectra, filters and equivalent baseband\n\n**Learning objective:** Understand waveform descriptions in both time and frequency, and define a consistent one-sided or two-sided spectral convention.\n\n## Physical perspective\n\nFor every signal, distinguish instantaneous amplitude, average power and energy. Sinusoidal examples may be power signals with infinite total energy, while finite pulses are finite-energy waveforms. The Fourier transform is a coordinate change that exposes filtering and channel occupancy. A filter that changes waveform shape also changes interference and noise, so there is no universal bandwidth independent of the measurement and the desired error rate.\n\n## Extended engineering case: how to reason from requirements\n\nThe foundation of spectral reasoning is to ask what a display actually computes. A laboratory spectrum analyzer can report power in a resolution bandwidth, a digital FFT routine can report a complex DFT coefficient, and a communications simulation may report a continuous density estimate. Those three views are not interchangeable until window energy, reference impedance, sample rate and calibration are specified. A sinusoid between FFT bins leaks across nearby estimates even if the underlying signal contains only one frequency. Increasing FFT size by zero-padding interpolates the same finite-record spectrum; it does not create additional observed information or improve fundamental resolution without longer data. Window tapering lowers distant sidelobes while changing the main-lobe width and noise bandwidth.\n\nThe LTI viewpoint unifies several devices: a copper cable, an acoustic room, a front-end anti-alias filter and a matched receiver filter. Each can be described locally as a convolution if its parameters do not change during the measurement. Apply a known test pulse and compare the observed shape with the impulse-response prediction. Then apply two tones with different phase. If a high-frequency tone is attenuated, the receiver may boost it but cannot undo noise that has already been added after attenuation. If two delayed components cancel exactly at a frequency, inverting the channel is ill-conditioned. A robust design often accepts some residual distortion to avoid extreme noise enhancement. Complex baseband is most useful when translating this reasoning from a 100 MHz carrier to a slowly varying envelope, but the sign convention and units must remain consistent.\n\nAs a quality audit, write a three-column notebook with time-domain prediction, spectral prediction and simulation observation. A correct simulation should satisfy each independent description within numerical tolerance. For example, a time delay of 2 ms appears as a linearly increasing negative phase with frequency, but preserves magnitude for an ideal delay-only channel. An amplitude notch implies a cancellation mechanism and cannot be inferred from phase slope alone.\n\n## Connections and study order\n\nThe next four lessons move from the simplest tractable model to a broader engineering treatment of this unit. Solve their worked cases separately first, then assemble the assumptions in a single coherent link design. For every step, distinguish data representing the actual message from a signal generated by the transceiver and from noise introduced by the environment or receiver. Use the model's limiting cases to decide whether parameters can be safely neglected. Finally, specify how any simulation could be validated through an independent analytic calculation and, if qualified laboratory access exists, through a supervised physical measurement.\n\n## Unit lessons\n\n- [2.1 Fourier transforms and spectral lines](../lessons/U02-L01-fourier-transforms-and-spectral-lines.html) \u2014 model `x(t)=A cos(2 pi f0 t) \u2194 (A/2)[\u03b4(f-f0)+\u03b4(f+f0)]`\n- [2.2 Sampling, aliasing and reconstruction](../lessons/U02-L02-sampling-aliasing-and-reconstruction.html) \u2014 model `fs>2B for a strictly bandlimited baseband signal with highest positive frequency B`\n- [2.3 Linear time-invariant channel and group delay](../lessons/U02-L03-linear-time-invariant-channel-and-group-delay.html) \u2014 model `y(t)=h(t)*x(t); H(f)=\u222bh(t)e^(-j2\u03c0ft)dt`\n- [2.4 Complex envelopes and I/Q representation](../lessons/U02-L04-complex-envelopes-and-i-q-representation.html) \u2014 model `s_RF(t)=Re{u(t)e^(j2\u03c0fct)} with complex envelope u(t)`\n\n## Unit assessment and project\n\n- [Self-assessment](../assessments/unit-02.html)\n- [Integrated project](../projects/unit-02.html)\n- [Interactive model](../simulations/model-02.html)\n"
