          "id": "sec-2-2",
          "secNumber": "2.2",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.1 Fourier transforms and spectral lines",
          "content": "# Lesson 2.1 Fourier transforms and spectral lines\n\n**Unit:** 2 \u2014 Signals, spectra, filters and equivalent baseband  \n**Core model:** `x(t)=A cos(2 pi f0 t) \u2194 (A/2)[\u03b4(f-f0)+\u03b4(f+f0)]`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nA single real sinusoid has conjugate-symmetric positive and negative Fourier components in the mathematical two-sided spectrum. Multiplying by a cosine produces shifted spectra; this is the mechanism behind upconversion and downconversion. Engineers sometimes show only a positive-frequency spectrum for a real radio signal, but the axis convention must be declared. For finite observation windows, tones appear as broadened peaks because multiplication by a time window convolves their ideal line spectrum with the window transform.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **fourier transforms and spectral lines**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `x(t)=A cos(2 pi f0 t) \u2194 (A/2)[\u03b4(f-f0)+\u03b4(f+f0)]`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nFor a 1 kHz sine observed over 10 ms, exactly ten cycles fit in the window. With a coherent sampled record, an FFT bin may align with the tone. Moving to 1.05 kHz while keeping a 10 ms record creates spectral leakage across bins.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nFFT-bin amplitude is not a calibrated power spectral density unless normalization, sample rate and window equivalent noise bandwidth are handled.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nGenerate tones whose frequencies do and do not fit an integer number of cycles into a record. Compare rectangular and Hann window frequency responses.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** FFT-bin amplitude is not a calibrated power spectral density unless normalization, sample rate and window equivalent noise bandwidth are handled. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Generate tones whose frequencies do and do not fit an integer number of cycles into a record. Compare rectangular and Hann window frequency responses. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **fourier transforms and spectral lines** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
