          "id": "sec-2-3",
          "secNumber": "2.3",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.2 Sampling, aliasing and reconstruction",
          "content": "# Lesson 2.2 Sampling, aliasing and reconstruction\n\n**Unit:** 2 \u2014 Signals, spectra, filters and equivalent baseband  \n**Core model:** `fs>2B for a strictly bandlimited baseband signal with highest positive frequency B`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nSampling repeats a continuous-time spectrum every sampling-frequency interval. If the replicas overlap, distinct analog inputs produce identical sample sequences and cannot be uniquely reconstructed. In practice, analog anti-alias filters limit the energy before the ADC, while finite aperture and clock jitter cause other impairments. The Nyquist statement is a sufficient sampling condition for a precisely bandlimited baseband waveform; bandpass sampling has a different set of feasibility constraints.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **sampling, aliasing and reconstruction**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `fs>2B for a strictly bandlimited baseband signal with highest positive frequency B`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nA 7 kHz tone sampled at 10 ksample/s produces the same discrete-time samples as a suitably phased 3 kHz alias. The observed folded frequency is |7-10|=3 kHz. Merely interpolating samples cannot reveal which original analog tone was present.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nDo not confuse the Nyquist frequency fs/2 with the filter transition width. Real filters need a guard band, and high-amplitude out-of-band signals may overload the front end.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nDesign a sampling plan for 0\u201320 kHz audio-like content. Specify a sample rate, anti-alias transition region and amplitude range.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** Do not confuse the Nyquist frequency fs/2 with the filter transition width. Real filters need a guard band, and high-amplitude out-of-band signals may overload the front end. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Design a sampling plan for 0\u201320 kHz audio-like content. Specify a sample rate, anti-alias transition region and amplitude range. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **sampling, aliasing and reconstruction** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
