          "heading": "Lesson 2.3 Linear time-invariant channel and group delay",
          "content": "# Lesson 2.3 Linear time-invariant channel and group delay\n\n**Unit:** 2 \u2014 Signals, spectra, filters and equivalent baseband  \n**Core model:** `y(t)=h(t)*x(t); H(f)=\u222bh(t)e^(-j2\u03c0ft)dt`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nAn LTI channel can attenuate, delay and disperse the constituent frequencies of an input. Its impulse response contains the same input\u2013output information as its transfer function, provided the model assumptions hold. Constant delay corresponds to a phase slope linear in frequency. If phase is nonlinear, spectral components travel with different group delays and the waveform can spread, increasing intersymbol interference even when the amplitude response seems flat. A causal system cannot depend on future inputs, and a physically stable impulse response should be compatible with the assumed energy or input\u2013output stability definition.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **linear time-invariant channel and group delay**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `y(t)=h(t)*x(t); H(f)=\u222bh(t)e^(-j2\u03c0ft)dt`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nTwo tones at 1 and 4 kHz pass through a channel with gains of 1 and 0.4 respectively. Even with zero added noise the output shape changes because high-frequency content is weaker. A subsequent equalizer can restore amplitude approximately while amplifying noise in the attenuated region.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nZero-forcing equalization is not always optimal: a spectral null makes inversion arbitrarily noise-sensitive. A perfectly rectangular channel frequency response also implies a noncausal or infinite-duration idealization.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nDraw magnitude and phase responses for a low-pass channel; propose a transmitted pulse and estimate how dispersion changes adjacent symbols.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** Zero-forcing equalization is not always optimal: a spectral null makes inversion arbitrarily noise-sensitive. A perfectly rectangular channel frequency response also implies a noncausal or infinite-duration idealization. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Draw magnitude and phase responses for a low-pass channel; propose a transmitted pulse and estimate how dispersion changes adjacent symbols. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **linear time-invariant channel and group delay** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
