          "id": "sec-2-5",
          "secNumber": "2.5",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.4 Complex envelopes and I/Q representation",
          "content": "# Lesson 2.4 Complex envelopes and I/Q representation\n\n**Unit:** 2 \u2014 Signals, spectra, filters and equivalent baseband  \n**Core model:** `s_RF(t)=Re{u(t)e^(j2\u03c0fct)} with complex envelope u(t)`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nA real passband radio signal can be represented through a complex envelope containing in-phase and quadrature parts. The complex representation is a mathematical convenience for analyzing amplitude and phase without numerically resolving every carrier cycle. If u=I+jQ, multiplying by a complex exponential rotates phase; time variation in phase creates frequency offsets. Practical I/Q modulators can have gain imbalance, quadrature error and DC offsets. Those impairments create image components, carrier leakage and biased constellation measurements.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **complex envelopes and i/q representation**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `s_RF(t)=Re{u(t)e^(j2\u03c0fct)} with complex envelope u(t)`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nIf u=0.6+j0.8, the magnitude is one and the envelope phase is atan2(0.8,0.6)\u224853.13 degrees. A 90 degree rotation gives -0.8+j0.6. The physical passband waveform remains real after taking the real part of the modulated complex exponential.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nThe sign convention for Q depends on whether an upconverter uses cos minus Q sin or cos plus Q sin. Never merge formulas with inconsistent conventions.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nSketch four QPSK baseband points, rotate them by a fixed carrier phase error and describe what a constellation display should show.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** The sign convention for Q depends on whether an upconverter uses cos minus Q sin or cos plus Q sin. Never merge formulas with inconsistent conventions. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Sketch four QPSK baseband points, rotate them by a fixed carrier phase error and describe what a constellation display should show. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **complex envelopes and i/q representation** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
