          "heading": "Theoretical Overview: Superheterodyne Receiver Architectures, Mixing, and Selectivity",
          "content": "# Unit 4: Angle modulation, FM and phase modulation\n\n**Learning objective:** Explain instantaneous phase and frequency and why constant-envelope signals can tolerate some amplitude variation.\n\n## Physical perspective\n\nFrequency and phase modulation encode a message as an angle trajectory rather than a direct amplitude fluctuation. This changes the receiver architecture: discriminators and phase-locked loops estimate derivatives or phase errors, while amplitude normalization can reduce some types of envelope interference. FM is not immune to noise; threshold effects, oscillator instability and bandwidth constraints remain important.\n\n## Extended engineering case: how to reason from requirements\n\nAngle modulation is a model of a changing phase trajectory. The instantaneous frequency formula is a derivative, and therefore exposes why high-frequency phase noise can be dangerous in a discriminator. The link between a sinusoidal modulating tone, frequency deviation and modulation index explains why larger deviation produces a greater number of significant FM sidebands. Constant transmitted amplitude is attractive for an efficient nonlinear power amplifier, but nonlinear hardware can still create spurious spectra if the phase trajectory or switching waveform is distorted. Amplitude limiting removes part of the received amplitude noise, not all the noise that affects the estimated phase.\n\nA simulation uses an FM carrier as if it were perfectly generated. A real radio also has oscillator phase noise, Doppler shift and time-varying attenuation. If the receiver is operated near the sensitivity limit, phase unwrapping may occasionally make a full-cycle mistake; an FM discriminator then produces a severe impulse. Such threshold behavior contradicts naive linearized noise calculations. Receiver filtering can reduce those impulses at the expense of rejecting abrupt legitimate message variation. Pre-emphasis and deemphasis operate on the *message* frequency weighting, not on free-space path loss.\n\nThe central engineering question is not 'Is FM always better than AM?' but 'Which disturbance, bandwidth limit and circuit complexity matter in this link?' Compare same received power, same message bandwidth and same nominal transmitter restrictions. Under a primarily multiplicative amplitude disturbance, FM with limiting often has a clear advantage; under frequency instability or narrow spectrum allocation that advantage can shrink. Give the comparison at least one explicit assumption in each sentence rather than relying on broadcasting folklore.\n\n## Connections and study order\n\nThe next four lessons move from the simplest tractable model to a broader engineering treatment of this unit. Solve their worked cases separately first, then assemble the assumptions in a single coherent link design. For every step, distinguish data representing the actual message from a signal generated by the transceiver and from noise introduced by the environment or receiver. Use the model's limiting cases to decide whether parameters can be safely neglected. Finally, specify how any simulation could be validated through an independent analytic calculation and, if qualified laboratory access exists, through a supervised physical measurement.\n\n## Unit lessons\n\n- [4.1 Instantaneous frequency and phase](../lessons/U04-L01-instantaneous-frequency-and-phase.html) \u2014 model `fi(t)=(1/2\u03c0)d\u03b8(t)/dt; \u03b8(t)=2\u03c0fct+2\u03c0kf\u222bm(t)dt`\n- [4.2 FM modulation index and Carson bandwidth](../lessons/U04-L02-fm-modulation-index-and-carson-bandwidth.html) \u2014 model `\u03b2=\u0394f/fm; approximate B\u22482(\u0394f+fm) for a single-tone FM planning estimate`\n- [4.3 FM demodulation and limiter-discriminator chains](../lessons/U04-L03-fm-demodulation-and-limiter-discriminator-chains.html) \u2014 model `demodulated message \u221d d/dt unwrap(arg(z(t))) after appropriate filtering`\n- [4.4 FM noise, pre-emphasis and practical tradeoffs](../lessons/U04-L04-fm-noise-pre-emphasis-and-practical-tradeoffs.html) \u2014 model `post-detection FM noise increases with baseband frequency under ideal limiting assumptions`\n\n## Unit assessment and project\n\n- [Self-assessment](../assessments/unit-04.html)\n- [Integrated project](../projects/unit-04.html)\n- [Interactive model](../simulations/model-04.html)\n"
