          "id": "sec-4-3",
          "secNumber": "4.3",
          "heading": "Lesson 4.2 FM modulation index and Carson bandwidth",
          "content": "# Lesson 4.2 FM modulation index and Carson bandwidth\n\n**Unit:** 4 \u2014 Angle modulation, FM and phase modulation  \n**Core model:** `\u03b2=\u0394f/fm; approximate B\u22482(\u0394f+fm) for a single-tone FM planning estimate`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nA sinusoidal frequency-modulating tone produces theoretically infinitely many sidebands with Bessel-function weights. For sufficiently small modulation index, first-order sidebands dominate; for larger index, many components matter. Carson\u2019s rule is a practical approximate occupancy measure, not a sharp spectral band limit or a legal emission mask. Frequency deviation and message bandwidth must be defined independently: \u03b2 for a single tone does not determine multitone occupancy without the message spectrum.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **fm modulation index and carson bandwidth**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `\u03b2=\u0394f/fm; approximate B\u22482(\u0394f+fm) for a single-tone FM planning estimate`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nAt peak deviation 75 kHz with a maximum message frequency of 15 kHz, Carson\u2019s estimate gives 2(75+15)=180 kHz. It is not a proof that spectral content is exactly zero beyond 90 kHz from carrier.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nA design must also address pre-emphasis, filtering, transmitter nonlinearity, and assigned channel constraints. Constant envelope helps power amplification but does not eliminate spurious emissions.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nCompute Carson estimates for fixed 5 kHz deviation at 1, 5 and 10 kHz modulation frequencies; then discuss how modulation index changes in the opposite direction.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** A design must also address pre-emphasis, filtering, transmitter nonlinearity, and assigned channel constraints. Constant envelope helps power amplification but does not eliminate spurious emissions. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Compute Carson estimates for fixed 5 kHz deviation at 1, 5 and 10 kHz modulation frequencies; then discuss how modulation index changes in the opposite direction. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **fm modulation index and carson bandwidth** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
