          "heading": "Lesson 4.3 FM demodulation and limiter-discriminator chains",
          "content": "# Lesson 4.3 FM demodulation and limiter-discriminator chains\n\n**Unit:** 4 \u2014 Angle modulation, FM and phase modulation  \n**Core model:** `demodulated message \u221d d/dt unwrap(arg(z(t))) after appropriate filtering`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nAn ideal FM receiver estimates the rate of change of phase, which is the message scaled by frequency sensitivity. Practical implementations may use slope detectors, quadrature detectors, frequency-to-voltage converters or phase-locked loops. A limiter suppresses some amplitude disturbance before frequency estimation, but abrupt amplitude fades can still cause large phase errors. Differentiation boosts high-frequency noise, motivating deemphasis and appropriate detection bandwidth. Digital quadrature receivers commonly compute phase differences between adjacent complex samples rather than unwrapping an entire noisy phase time series.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **fm demodulation and limiter-discriminator chains**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `demodulated message \u221d d/dt unwrap(arg(z(t))) after appropriate filtering`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nSuppose phase advances 0.08 rad per sample at a 100 ksample/s complex sample rate. The corresponding frequency offset is 0.08\u00b7100000/(2\u03c0)\u22481273 Hz, provided phase increments stay in an unambiguous range.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nDiscrete phase differences wrap into [-\u03c0,\u03c0); if frequency change between samples is too large, aliasing of the estimated phase derivative occurs.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nSimulate a 1 kHz modulating tone, apply 5 kHz peak deviation and inspect the discriminator output with and without noise and envelope fades.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** Discrete phase differences wrap into [-\u03c0,\u03c0); if frequency change between samples is too large, aliasing of the estimated phase derivative occurs. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Simulate a 1 kHz modulating tone, apply 5 kHz peak deviation and inspect the discriminator output with and without noise and envelope fades. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **fm demodulation and limiter-discriminator chains** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
