          "id": "sec-4-5",
          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Lesson 4.4 FM noise, pre-emphasis and practical tradeoffs",
          "content": "# Lesson 4.4 FM noise, pre-emphasis and practical tradeoffs\n\n**Unit:** 4 \u2014 Angle modulation, FM and phase modulation  \n**Core model:** `post-detection FM noise increases with baseband frequency under ideal limiting assumptions`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nAbove the FM threshold, a high-SNR approximation can make demodulated noise grow with message frequency because frequency detection differentiates phase. Pre-emphasis boosts selected high-frequency message components before FM, while a complementary deemphasis network at the receiver reduces their relative noise. The network shapes must be paired and must not drive the transmitter beyond allowed peak deviation. Near weak-signal threshold, capture effects and rapidly changing phase lead to behavior not predicted by a simple high-SNR linear approximation.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **fm noise, pre-emphasis and practical tradeoffs**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `post-detection FM noise increases with baseband frequency under ideal limiting assumptions`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nIf a deemphasis network has time constant 75 microseconds, its first-order corner is approximately 1/(2\u03c0\u00b775 \u03bcs)=2.12 kHz. This is a characterization of the filter, not a guarantee that a particular broadcasting standard applies.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nImproving recovered high-frequency SNR by pre-emphasis can increase worst-case deviation for sharp transitions. Specifications must define the permitted modulation spectrum.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nFor two otherwise identical links, compare a limiter/discriminator receiver and an AM envelope detector when interference is primarily amplitude fluctuation. Explain the assumptions behind the comparison.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** Improving recovered high-frequency SNR by pre-emphasis can increase worst-case deviation for sharp transitions. Specifications must define the permitted modulation spectrum. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** For two otherwise identical links, compare a limiter/discriminator receiver and an AM envelope detector when interference is primarily amplitude fluctuation. Explain the assumptions behind the comparison. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **fm noise, pre-emphasis and practical tradeoffs** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
