          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Superheterodyne Receiver Architectures, Mixing, and Selectivity",
          "content": "# Unit 4 assessment \u2014 Angle modulation, FM and phase modulation\n\n**Instructions:** show the reference planes, numerical steps and units. These are explanatory solution outlines, not a substitute for deriving the equations. Allocate 100 points: 20 each for Questions 1\u20134 and 20 for the integration problem.\n\n## Question 1: Instantaneous frequency and phase (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** With frequency sensitivity 5 kHz per volt and a +0.4 V constant message, an FM carrier shifts by +2 kHz while the message remains constant. For sinusoidal message, peak frequency deviation is kf times peak message voltage. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `fi(t)=(1/2\u03c0)d\u03b8(t)/dt; \u03b8(t)=2\u03c0fct+2\u03c0kf\u222bm(t)dt`. Do not interchange sensitivity in Hz/V and sensitivity in rad/s/V. Confusing those units inserts a factor of 2\u03c0 in an FM design. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 2: FM modulation index and Carson bandwidth (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** At peak deviation 75 kHz with a maximum message frequency of 15 kHz, Carson\u2019s estimate gives 2(75+15)=180 kHz. It is not a proof that spectral content is exactly zero beyond 90 kHz from carrier. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `\u03b2=\u0394f/fm; approximate B\u22482(\u0394f+fm) for a single-tone FM planning estimate`. A design must also address pre-emphasis, filtering, transmitter nonlinearity, and assigned channel constraints. Constant envelope helps power amplification but does not eliminate spurious emissions. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 3: FM demodulation and limiter-discriminator chains (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** Suppose phase advances 0.08 rad per sample at a 100 ksample/s complex sample rate. The corresponding frequency offset is 0.08\u00b7100000/(2\u03c0)\u22481273 Hz, provided phase increments stay in an unambiguous range. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `demodulated message \u221d d/dt unwrap(arg(z(t))) after appropriate filtering`. Discrete phase differences wrap into [-\u03c0,\u03c0); if frequency change between samples is too large, aliasing of the estimated phase derivative occurs. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 4: FM noise, pre-emphasis and practical tradeoffs (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** If a deemphasis network has time constant 75 microseconds, its first-order corner is approximately 1/(2\u03c0\u00b775 \u03bcs)=2.12 kHz. This is a characterization of the filter, not a guarantee that a particular broadcasting standard applies. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `post-detection FM noise increases with baseband frequency under ideal limiting assumptions`. Improving recovered high-frequency SNR by pre-emphasis can increase worst-case deviation for sharp transitions. Specifications must define the permitted modulation spectrum. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 5: integrate the unit into a receiver chain (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** Propose a complete signal chain for the unit's principal application. Show where source content, oscillator/reference information, channel distortion, random noise and decision logic enter. For each of the four lesson mechanisms, label an observable that a student could calculate or simulate. Choose one validation check independent of the simulation itself.\n\n**Solution outline.** A strong answer includes a correctly ordered block diagram, a table of input/output units, a resource budget (power, spectrum or sampling), and at least one numerical limiting case. A student's independent calculation must use the same model conventions as the interactive graph. Discuss which missing impairment could overturn the proposed design. The answer should explain why a locally accurate mechanism does not guarantee successful end-to-end information delivery.\n\n**Marking:** 5 points for the architecture, 5 for model consistency, 5 for independent validation, 5 for credible limitations.\n"
