          "id": "sec-5-3",
          "secNumber": "5.3",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.2 Uniform quantization and SQNR",
          "content": "# Lesson 5.2 Uniform quantization and SQNR\n\n**Unit:** 5 \u2014 Pulse modulation, PCM and quantization  \n**Core model:** `\u0394=(Vmax-Vmin)/2^N; high-resolution sine SQNR\u22486.02N+1.76 dB under specified assumptions`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nA uniform quantizer maps each analog input in an allowed range into a finite reproduction level. The maximum granular error for a nearest-neighbor midrise rule is roughly half a step, provided the signal stays within range. Treating quantization error as a uniform, independent random process is an approximation that can fail for tiny coherent inputs or overload. The common 6.02N+1.76 dB sine-wave expression assumes a full-scale ideal sinusoid, high resolution, no overload and sufficiently decorrelated error. It is not a general ADC SNR guarantee.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **uniform quantization and sqnr**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `\u0394=(Vmax-Vmin)/2^N; high-resolution sine SQNR\u22486.02N+1.76 dB under specified assumptions`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nFor an 8-bit uniform quantizer spanning -1 to +1 V, \u0394=2/256=7.8125 mV. The idealized full-scale sine SQNR estimate is 6.02\u00b78+1.76\u224849.92 dB.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nA quiet signal far below full scale has worse relative SQNR unless gain, companding or noise shaping is used. Dither can linearize distortion at the cost of extra noise.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nQuantize a full-scale sine and a sine with one-quarter the amplitude. Compare measured errors to the ideal formula and explain why the absolute step remains unchanged.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** A quiet signal far below full scale has worse relative SQNR unless gain, companding or noise shaping is used. Dither can linearize distortion at the cost of extra noise. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Quantize a full-scale sine and a sine with one-quarter the amplitude. Compare measured errors to the ideal formula and explain why the absolute step remains unchanged. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **uniform quantization and sqnr** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
