          "secNumber": "5.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.3 Pulse-code modulation rate and framing",
          "content": "# Lesson 5.3 Pulse-code modulation rate and framing\n\n**Unit:** 5 \u2014 Pulse modulation, PCM and quantization  \n**Core model:** `Rb=fs Nbits nchannels before compression or transport overhead`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nPCM typically means sampling, quantizing and labeling a waveform in binary. The raw source rate follows directly from samples per second, bits per sample and channel count. An actual stream also needs timing, frame boundaries, error checks and possibly multiplexing. A designer should distinguish aggregate bit rate from net application payload rate; compression can reduce one while introducing processing delay and potentially distortion. Time-division multiplexing interleaves channel data and must preserve channel identity and timing.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **pulse-code modulation rate and framing**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `Rb=fs Nbits nchannels before compression or transport overhead`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nStereo audio sampled at 48 ksample/s with 16 bits per sample yields 48,000\u00b716\u00b72=1,536,000 bits/s before framing. A system offering 1 Mb/s usable throughput cannot carry this raw PCM stream without compression or rate reduction.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nA packet loss is not a quantization error. Repeating packets may improve delivery integrity but cannot reconstruct samples that were never captured correctly.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nDesign a PCM data frame for four sensor channels with different sample rates. Specify how timestamps avoid ambiguity when one sensor updates less often.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** A packet loss is not a quantization error. Repeating packets may improve delivery integrity but cannot reconstruct samples that were never captured correctly. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Design a PCM data frame for four sensor channels with different sample rates. Specify how timestamps avoid ambiguity when one sensor updates less often. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **pulse-code modulation rate and framing** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
