          "id": "sec-5-6",
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Noise in Analog Continuous-Wave Communication Systems (SNR, Noise Figure)",
          "content": "# Unit 5 project \u2014 Pulse modulation, PCM and quantization: an auditable synthetic design study\n\n**Problem statement.** Connect finite-rate digital sources to sampling, quantization and framing and distinguish quantizer noise from channel errors. The deliverable is a self-contained engineering analysis, not a claim that a device was measured or certified.\n\n## Scenario\n\nDigitization should be audited as a cascade of distinct irreversible decisions. The anti-alias filter is chosen before sampling; it rejects analog content that the sample rate cannot uniquely represent. Sampling then produces time-indexed amplitudes. Quantization reduces the amplitude alphabet to a finite set, creating a controlled but irreversible error. Binary labeling selects how those levels are represented in bits. Framing establishes how a receiver finds the sample boundaries and the channel identity. Bit errors introduced later by the communication channel are different from quantizer error introduced at the source.\n\n## Required work products\n\n1. **Requirements document.** State the message type, sample or symbol rate, occupied-bandwidth convention, reference impedances or powers and a numerical reliability criterion. Differentiate functional requirements (what users need) from implementation choices (how blocks operate).\n2. **Equation notebook.** For each of the unit's four governing relationships, derive the formula assumptions, provide an annotated worked calculation and evaluate a limiting case. Keep all intermediate calculations in linear units until a logarithmic representation is actually necessary.\n3. **Simulation experiment.** Open the associated synthetic model. Take screenshots or download CSV for three input settings: nominal, stressed, and nearly ideal. Record why each setting was chosen. Compare at least two numerical points with a direct formula calculation independent of the browser code.\n4. **Failure analysis.** Write one paragraph each on systematic bias, random variation and model mismatch. State the measurement instrument or independent experiment needed to distinguish the alternatives in a supervised hardware implementation.\n5. **Decision memo.** Recommend a configuration for the stated application. Include a tradeoff table of bandwidth, power, complexity, delay, expected errors and uncertainty. Explicitly identify the two most consequential unverified assumptions.\n\n## Suggested numerical investigation\n\n- **Experiment 1, Pulse amplitude modulation and time-domain reconstruction:** Compare rectangular and raised-cosine-like pulse shapes. Observe the difference between sampled symbol values and the continuous-time waveform between decisions. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 2, Uniform quantization and SQNR:** Quantize a full-scale sine and a sine with one-quarter the amplitude. Compare measured errors to the ideal formula and explain why the absolute step remains unchanged. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 3, Pulse-code modulation rate and framing:** Design a PCM data frame for four sensor channels with different sample rates. Specify how timestamps avoid ambiguity when one sensor updates less often. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 4, Companding, delta modulation and overload:** Compare a histogram concentrated near zero with a uniform distribution. Explain why the better quantizer depends on the source and distortion measure rather than bit count alone. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n\n## Evaluation rubric (100 points)\n\n- Model derivations and units: 25 points. A formula with unexplained symbols does not earn full marks.\n- Independent arithmetic and numerical consistency: 20 points. Reproduce at least one graph point without reusing the graph's implementation.\n- Simulation experiment and transparent provenance: 20 points. Identify every result as synthetic.\n- Failure analysis and sensitivity: 20 points. Include meaningful nonideal tests, not only cosmetic parameter changes.\n- Clear engineering recommendation: 15 points. Tie the recommendation to the defined objective and state where further evidence is required.\n\n**Safety:** do not build or operate radio transmitters, powered antennas or high-voltage test apparatus as part of this offline project.\n"
