          "id": "sec-6-5",
          "secNumber": "6.5",
          "heading": "Lesson 6.4 Multipath, fading and channel coherence",
          "content": "# Lesson 6.4 Multipath, fading and channel coherence\n\n**Unit:** 6 \u2014 Random processes, noise and communication channels  \n**Core model:** `r(t)=\u2211k ak x(t-\u03c4k)+n(t) for a tapped delay-line channel`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nA physical receiver often collects delayed copies of the same transmitted waveform. Their complex phasors may reinforce or cancel at different frequencies, producing frequency-selective fading. Channel coherence bandwidth is related to delay spread, while coherence time is related to Doppler spread; both relationships require a precise definition and statistical channel model. A flat-fading approximation is plausible only if the occupied signal bandwidth is small relative to channel variation in frequency. Equalization, diversity and adaptive coding address distinct risks.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **multipath, fading and channel coherence**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `r(t)=\u2211k ak x(t-\u03c4k)+n(t) for a tapped delay-line channel`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nTwo equal-amplitude paths with a 1 microsecond relative delay interfere constructively at some frequencies and destructively where phase difference equals odd multiples of \u03c0. The approximate notch spacing between equivalent destructive conditions is 1/\u03c4=1 MHz.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nA deterministic two-tap example is not a Rayleigh or Rician statistical fading model unless a distribution for the path coefficients has been declared.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nFor 100 ns and 1 \u03bcs path separation, sketch magnitude versus frequency and predict which case creates more rapid frequency ripples.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** A deterministic two-tap example is not a Rayleigh or Rician statistical fading model unless a distribution for the path coefficients has been declared. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** For 100 ns and 1 \u03bcs path separation, sketch magnitude versus frequency and predict which case creates more rapid frequency ripples. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **multipath, fading and channel coherence** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
