          "id": "sec-6-7",
          "secNumber": "6.7",
          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Sampling Theorem, Pulse Amplitude Modulation (PAM), and Time-Division Multiplexing",
          "content": "# Unit 6 assessment \u2014 Random processes, noise and communication channels\n\n**Instructions:** show the reference planes, numerical steps and units. These are explanatory solution outlines, not a substitute for deriving the equations. Allocate 100 points: 20 each for Questions 1\u20134 and 20 for the integration problem.\n\n## Question 1: Probability, autocorrelation and power spectra (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** If independent zero-mean samples have variance 0.04 V\u00b2, their lag-zero correlation equals 0.04 V\u00b2 and off-zero discrete-time correlation approaches zero in the ensemble model. Individual finite records still show accidental nonzero estimates. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `Rx(\u03c4)=E[x(t)x*(t-\u03c4)]; Sx(f)=F{Rx(\u03c4)} for WSS processes`. Never interpret a periodogram estimate as the exact underlying PSD. Window choice, averaging, record length and one-sided/two-sided scaling affect numerical values. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 2: Thermal noise, matched filtering and equivalent noise bandwidth (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** An ideal one-sided thermal noise density of -174 dBm/Hz integrated over 1 MHz gives approximately -114 dBm before receiver noise figure. A 6 dB noise figure makes input-referred noise about -108 dBm under matched reference conditions. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `Nout=\u222bS_n(f)|H(f)|\u00b2df using compatible two-sided conventions`. Filter bandwidth should be defined at the point in the system where noise and signal are being compared. A matched filter does not imply infinitely sharp stopband rejection or zero timing sensitivity. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 3: AWGN, Eb/N0 and probability of error (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** At Eb/N0=6 dB, linear ratio is 3.981. The ideal BPSK bit-error probability is Q(\u221a7.962)=Q(2.822)\u22480.00239. This does not say a short burst will contain exactly 0.239% errors. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `for coherent BPSK in AWGN, Pb=Q(sqrt(2Eb/N0))`. Comparing BER curves requires the same coded versus uncoded bit-energy convention. Receiver estimates can also be biased if synchronization errors are ignored. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 4: Multipath, fading and channel coherence (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** Two equal-amplitude paths with a 1 microsecond relative delay interfere constructively at some frequencies and destructively where phase difference equals odd multiples of \u03c0. The approximate notch spacing between equivalent destructive conditions is 1/\u03c4=1 MHz. Reconstruct the calculation or argument and then choose a plausible change of one operating variable. Explain the direction and approximate magnitude of the result without assuming the model applies outside its stated range. Give one separate observation that would invalidate the ideal prediction.\n\n**Solution outline.** Begin with `r(t)=\u2211k ak x(t-\u03c4k)+n(t) for a tapped delay-line channel`. A deterministic two-tap example is not a Rayleigh or Rician statistical fading model unless a distribution for the path coefficients has been declared. Full credit requires the value or directional consequence, defined units, and explicit treatment of the limiting assumptions. A correct numerical answer without a meaningful reference plane receives at most half credit; a diagram without a justified computation also receives partial credit only.\n\n## Question 5: integrate the unit into a receiver chain (20 points)\n\n**Problem.** Propose a complete signal chain for the unit's principal application. Show where source content, oscillator/reference information, channel distortion, random noise and decision logic enter. For each of the four lesson mechanisms, label an observable that a student could calculate or simulate. Choose one validation check independent of the simulation itself.\n\n**Solution outline.** A strong answer includes a correctly ordered block diagram, a table of input/output units, a resource budget (power, spectrum or sampling), and at least one numerical limiting case. A student's independent calculation must use the same model conventions as the interactive graph. Discuss which missing impairment could overturn the proposed design. The answer should explain why a locally accurate mechanism does not guarantee successful end-to-end information delivery.\n\n**Marking:** 5 points for the architecture, 5 for model consistency, 5 for independent validation, 5 for credible limitations.\n"
