          "heading": "Lesson 8.2 BPSK and matched-filter decisions",
          "content": "# Lesson 8.2 BPSK and matched-filter decisions\n\n**Unit:** 8 \u2014 Coherent digital modulation and detection foundations  \n**Core model:** `BPSK symbols \u00b1sqrt(Eb) in normalized signal space`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nBPSK uses opposite phases of the same carrier or equivalent baseband pulse. With perfect coherent phase reference and symmetric AWGN, the maximum-likelihood decision is simply the sign of the matched-filter projection. Normalizing signal-space coordinates makes geometric distance explicit: the points lie 2\u221aEb apart. An unknown 180-degree phase ambiguity swaps every bit, which motivates pilots, differential encoding or a code structure capable of detecting inversion. The BER function assumes the receiver samples at the intended pulse time.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **bpsk and matched-filter decisions**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `BPSK symbols \u00b1sqrt(Eb) in normalized signal space`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nAt Eb=1 normalized unit and N0=0.5, Eb/N0=2 (3.01 dB). Ideal coherent BPSK bit-error rate is Q(2)\u22480.02275. Raising energy by 3 dB improves performance nonlinearly, not by a fixed additive BER amount.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nA hard-decision detector discards reliability information that a soft-input decoder could exploit. The simple sign rule also fails with nonzero decision bias or correlated interference.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nSimulate a scatter of noisy symbols along the real axis. Record how often samples fall on the wrong side of zero at several Eb/N0 levels.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** A hard-decision detector discards reliability information that a soft-input decoder could exploit. The simple sign rule also fails with nonzero decision bias or correlated interference. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Simulate a scatter of noisy symbols along the real axis. Record how often samples fall on the wrong side of zero at several Eb/N0 levels. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **bpsk and matched-filter decisions** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
