          "secNumber": "8.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 8.3 QPSK, quadrature carrier mixing and symbol mapping",
          "content": "# Lesson 8.3 QPSK, quadrature carrier mixing and symbol mapping\n\n**Unit:** 8 \u2014 Coherent digital modulation and detection foundations  \n**Core model:** `QPSK symbols (\u00b11\u00b1j)/sqrt(2); Es=2Eb for uncoded equal-probability mapping`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nQPSK conveys two coded bits per symbol using orthogonal I and Q components. With a Gray mapping, nearest-neighbor symbol errors usually change one bit, improving BER relative to arbitrary bit assignments at moderate noise. Under equal energy per bit, ideal coherent Gray-coded QPSK in AWGN has the same bit-error probability as BPSK, despite transmitting twice as many bits per symbol. Spectral occupation also depends on pulse shaping, not only the number of constellation points.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **qpsk, quadrature carrier mixing and symbol mapping**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `QPSK symbols (\u00b11\u00b1j)/sqrt(2); Es=2Eb for uncoded equal-probability mapping`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nTo transmit 100 kb/s uncoded with QPSK, nominal symbol rate is 50 ksym/s. With raised-cosine roll-off \u03b1=0.25, the idealized complex-baseband occupied bandwidth scale is Rs(1+\u03b1)=62.5 kHz; be explicit about passband or complex-baseband width convention.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nA rotated constellation can look like a different mapping. Clock recovery and carrier recovery must be evaluated before blaming random noise for systematic symbol errors.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nRotate four points by 22.5 degrees and add Gaussian noise. Compare a fixed-axis slicer with a receiver that estimates and removes the known rotation.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** A rotated constellation can look like a different mapping. Clock recovery and carrier recovery must be evaluated before blaming random noise for systematic symbol errors. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Rotate four points by 22.5 degrees and add Gaussian noise. Compare a fixed-axis slicer with a receiver that estimates and removes the known rotation. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **qpsk, quadrature carrier mixing and symbol mapping** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
