          "heading": "Lesson 8.4 Pulse shaping, Nyquist ISI criterion and eye diagrams",
          "content": "# Lesson 8.4 Pulse shaping, Nyquist ISI criterion and eye diagrams\n\n**Unit:** 8 \u2014 Coherent digital modulation and detection foundations  \n**Core model:** `ideal raised-cosine passband width \u2248Rs(1+\u03b1) with rolloff \u03b1 in [0,1]`  \n**Suggested study:** derive the model, solve its worked case, compare one limiting case, then attempt the open questions.\n\n## What is being described?\n\nA zero-ISI pulse has the property that its sampled values at symbol-spaced offsets vanish except at the origin, under ideal synchronization. Raised-cosine pulses are common examples, with \u03b1 trading spectral compactness against sharper time-domain sidelobes. In many transmitters a root-raised-cosine filter is used, and a matching receiver filter produces the overall raised-cosine response. An eye diagram superimposes symbol intervals to reveal timing margin and interference. The eye is a diagnostic, not a direct absolute BER certificate.\n\n## Derivation and interpretation\n\nBegin by writing the input, output and channel reference planes for **pulse shaping, nyquist isi criterion and eye diagrams**. The governing relationship in this lesson is `ideal raised-cosine passband width \u2248Rs(1+\u03b1) with rolloff \u03b1 in [0,1]`. Read each symbol according to its definition in the accompanying discussion, not as a context-free calculator instruction. Where the formula uses decibels, convert back to linear power before adding contributions. Where it describes a spectrum, identify whether positive and negative frequencies, peak or RMS amplitudes and window normalization are being used. If a formula describes probability, specify the random variable and statistical model. These conditions are substantive mathematical requirements: using the right arithmetic on the wrong reference conditions does not produce a correct engineering result.\n\n## Worked engineering case\n\nFor Rs=200 ksym/s and \u03b1=0.35, the usual ideal raised-cosine null-to-null passband bandwidth estimate is 270 kHz. Actual emissions extend beyond finite filters and must be compared against spectral-mask measurements.\n\n## Failure mode and model boundary\n\nLower \u03b1 does not always improve a real link: narrow pulse transitions are more sensitive to timing errors and demand longer filter impulse responses.\n\n## Investigation and design transfer\n\nSweep rolloff factor and timing error in a synthetic pulse-shaping display. Identify when the eye is widest at the receiver sampling instant.\n\n## Questions and solution guidance\n\n1. **Derivation:** Starting from the stated governing relation, define the variables and derive or justify the result under its intended assumptions. **Guidance:** show every conversion, distinguish the signal and noise reference planes, and explain why each approximation holds.\n2. **Check the worked example:** Recompute the numerical case, then change one parameter by a factor of two. **Guidance:** perform the change in linear units when powers or probabilities are involved; translate to decibels only at the end, if appropriate.\n3. **Challenge the assumption:** Lower \u03b1 does not always improve a real link: narrow pulse transitions are more sensitive to timing errors and demand longer filter impulse responses. **Guidance:** identify the precise assumption being challenged and propose an observable counter-test rather than treating the limitation as a generic warning.\n4. **Engineering design:** Sweep rolloff factor and timing error in a synthetic pulse-shaping display. Identify when the eye is widest at the receiver sampling instant. **Guidance:** specify achievable measurements, independent variables, expected plots and a criterion for accepting or rejecting the design.\n\n## Connection to the rest of the chain\n\nA communication receiver does not experience this mechanism in isolation. Source statistics influence coding; waveform choice influences occupied bandwidth; filtering changes the noise entering the decision statistic; and synchronization transforms signal geometry into errors. When using the result from **pulse shaping, nyquist isi criterion and eye diagrams** inside an end-to-end budget, name its upstream inputs and downstream consequences. Avoid adding an improvement twice: reducing noise bandwidth and improving SNR through a filter may describe the same physical change. Conversely, an unmodeled impairment can create a gap between a correct local calculation and actual delivered payload. A full design review therefore asks for both local correctness and end-to-end error or outage evidence.\n\n"
