          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Digital Baseband Transmission, Inter-Symbol Interference (ISI), and Nyquist Criterion",
          "content": "# Unit 8 project \u2014 Coherent digital modulation and detection foundations: an auditable synthetic design study\n\n**Problem statement.** Introduce coherent ASK/BPSK/QPSK, constellation decisions, pulse shaping and timing recovery. The deliverable is a self-contained engineering analysis, not a claim that a device was measured or certified.\n\n## Scenario\n\nA constellation is not a picture of electromagnetic radiation traveling through space. It is the receiver's collection of noisy coordinates after filtering, frequency translation and timing selection. The ideal points are deliberately separated so the detector can use geometry. In AWGN with equal noise variance on orthogonal axes, nearest-point detection is maximum likelihood for equal priors. A carrier-phase error rotates the point cloud; an I/Q gain imbalance stretches it, a DC offset shifts it and timing errors smear the clusters. Correcting only SNR cannot repair those systematic effects.\n\n## Required work products\n\n1. **Requirements document.** State the message type, sample or symbol rate, occupied-bandwidth convention, reference impedances or powers and a numerical reliability criterion. Differentiate functional requirements (what users need) from implementation choices (how blocks operate).\n2. **Equation notebook.** For each of the unit's four governing relationships, derive the formula assumptions, provide an annotated worked calculation and evaluate a limiting case. Keep all intermediate calculations in linear units until a logarithmic representation is actually necessary.\n3. **Simulation experiment.** Open the associated synthetic model. Take screenshots or download CSV for three input settings: nominal, stressed, and nearly ideal. Record why each setting was chosen. Compare at least two numerical points with a direct formula calculation independent of the browser code.\n4. **Failure analysis.** Write one paragraph each on systematic bias, random variation and model mismatch. State the measurement instrument or independent experiment needed to distinguish the alternatives in a supervised hardware implementation.\n5. **Decision memo.** Recommend a configuration for the stated application. Include a tradeoff table of bandwidth, power, complexity, delay, expected errors and uncertainty. Explicitly identify the two most consequential unverified assumptions.\n\n## Suggested numerical investigation\n\n- **Experiment 1, Binary ASK, OOK and energy detection:** Plot false-alarm and missed-detection probabilities as threshold moves across two Gaussian distributions. Find a threshold for equal priors and discuss the effect of asymmetric costs. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 2, BPSK and matched-filter decisions:** Simulate a scatter of noisy symbols along the real axis. Record how often samples fall on the wrong side of zero at several Eb/N0 levels. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 3, QPSK, quadrature carrier mixing and symbol mapping:** Rotate four points by 22.5 degrees and add Gaussian noise. Compare a fixed-axis slicer with a receiver that estimates and removes the known rotation. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n- **Experiment 4, Pulse shaping, Nyquist ISI criterion and eye diagrams:** Sweep rolloff factor and timing error in a synthetic pulse-shaping display. Identify when the eye is widest at the receiver sampling instant. Translate the experiment into three adjustable parameters; record predicted trend and one case that would challenge it.\n\n## Evaluation rubric (100 points)\n\n- Model derivations and units: 25 points. A formula with unexplained symbols does not earn full marks.\n- Independent arithmetic and numerical consistency: 20 points. Reproduce at least one graph point without reusing the graph's implementation.\n- Simulation experiment and transparent provenance: 20 points. Identify every result as synthetic.\n- Failure analysis and sensitivity: 20 points. Include meaningful nonideal tests, not only cosmetic parameter changes.\n- Clear engineering recommendation: 15 points. Tie the recommendation to the defined objective and state where further evidence is required.\n\n**Safety:** do not build or operate radio transmitters, powered antennas or high-voltage test apparatus as part of this offline project.\n"
